One thing I learned from this week’s readings was how Git and GitHub use version
control to keep track of changes to a project. Before the readings, I did 
not fully understand how branches worked or why they are useful. I learned that
branches allow you to make changes to a project without immediately changing the
main version. Commits also create a history of the changes that have been made 
which makes it possible to keep track of how a project progresses over time.

One challenge I faced during this assignment was understanding how to tell 
whether I was editing the README file on the new branch or the version on the 
main branch. When I opened the repository in File Explorer, there was still 
only one README file, so I initially thought I might be editing the same file. 
I learned that Git does not create a separate folder or visible copy of the 
file for each branch. Instead, GitHub Desktop shows which branch is currently 
checked out, and the files in the repository reflect that branch.

I can see GitHub being useful for future team analytics projects because 
multiple people can work on different parts of a project without directly 
changing the main version. For example, team members could use separate branches 
for data cleaning, analysis, or documentation and then review the changes before 
merging them onto the main branch. This would make it easier to keep each 
person's work organized while also keeping a history of the project.